# Environment
.venv/
.venv-pip/
uv.lock.bak

# The supply-chain tooling's own environment, kept apart from .venv so that
# pip-audit's 29 transitive packages never enter the environment the gate
# type-checks and tests against (.github/requirements/audit.txt).
.venv-audit/

# Build
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Tool caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Never commit a review workspace. It lives in ~/.security-review (STACK.md §6)
# and must never appear here; this line exists to catch a stray --workspace .
/.security-review/

# The OS writes this one whether or not anyone opts in, which is what separates
# it from an editor directory. macOS is a supported platform and CI runs on
# macos-latest; Finder drops a .DS_Store into every directory it opens, and one
# inside tests/fixtures/ changes what a golden test traverses and hashes (NFR-3).
.DS_Store

# git's own conflict leftovers. A committed *.orig is a second, stale copy of a
# file that the tool would then inventory and sweep as though it were real.
*.orig
*.rej

# Credentials. settings.json already denies reading these; this keeps a stray one
# from being committed. A scanner that leaks a .env is not a scanner.
.env
.env.*

# The product gate's success marker. Machine-local and per-checkout by design:
# it records that *this* working tree passed, and a committed one would tell
# every clone that it had. Written by scripts/check.sh, read by the harness —
# the marker crosses the boundary in the readable direction only.
.gate-passed

# The audit environment (STACK.md §2.2). Kept out of .venv on purpose: pip-audit
# and pip-licenses bring a network client stack, and the environment that
# type-checks and tests the code stays the packages someone chose.
.venv-audit/
